 def eq(tex,text):b.append(dict(kind='equation',tex=tex,text=text))
 def page():b.append(dict(kind='page'))
 def metric(cohort,n):return next(m for m in s['cohort_metrics'] if m['cohort']==cohort and m['policy']==n)
 h('Abstract')
 p('GUI grounding models can return plausible coordinates when an instruction has no visible or unique target. We audit native presence, match-count and box-confidence signals through synthetic layouts and independently human-labeled real interfaces. A provenance audit removes reused pilot material from the transfer analysis, leaving 751 real-interface cases. On these cases, a three-signal gate accepts 26 actions with 61.5% conditional error risk; the frozen logistic gate accepts only one action. Strict localization succeeds on 31.3% of unique targets. A matched-input quantized Qwen baseline provides a separate test of localization and prompted semantic abstention. Label and geometry sensitivity analyses distinguish annotation uncertainty from base-grounder and gate limitations. Real-data recalibration improves probability loss but does not recover a qualifying low-risk operating point under a fixed Learn-then-Test procedure. Native gating succeeds on authored layouts, yet useful automation remains unestablished on this real-interface cohort. We provide traceable source audits, cluster-based uncertainty estimates and reproducible decision analysis; no deployment safety guarantee is claimed.')
 h('1. Introduction and research questions')
 p('A screen coordinate answers where a model would click; it does not establish whether the requested control exists or is uniquely identified. Missing, duplicated, occluded and offscreen targets create different decision problems. We study a pinned native visual interface that returns presence, a caller-defined match-count distribution, and one bounding box in a single request.')
 p('RQ1: Do native presence and uniqueness scores reduce incorrect accepted clicks relative to box confidence on held-out synthetic layouts? RQ2: How well do the frozen policies transfer to human-labeled real interfaces? RQ3: How do coverage, localization, calibration, latency and cost change across sources and target states? RQ4: How does a frozen local VLM baseline perform on the same images and human references? The contribution is a reproducible audit of a native model interface, its transfer limitations and a matched-input baseline. We do not propose a new uncertainty-control algorithm.')
 h('2. Related work and evidence boundaries')
 p('OS-ATLAS develops cross-platform GUI grounding and action modeling [1]. SafeGround uses stochastic spatial dispersion and calibration to control false discoveries [2]. Argus compares uncertainty methods across models and interfaces, finding that transfer depends on the observable regime [3]. CORA learns an action-risk guardian and calibrates execute/abstain behavior [4]. Selective classification [10], Learn then Test [11] and Conformal Risk Control [12] supply the foundational rejection and risk-control framework. Qwen2.5-VL [13], ScreenSpot-Pro [14] and UI-TARS [15] provide additional GUI-model and benchmark context. These establish prior art for abstention, calibration and GUI safety. Our narrower evidence concerns three native scores from Jev 1.14 and the limitations of a synthetic-to-real evaluation. Published VLM performance is contextualized in Appendix D rather than treated as an experiment on our labels.')
 h('3. Model interface, policies and mathematical setup')
 p('Every primary-study request pins jev-1.14.0 with prompt presence-count-box-v1; Appendix C.4 separately specifies the repeat and prompt-variant protocol. Let p be visible-presence confidence, u the Choice probability of exactly one match, and b box confidence. The parser rejects malformed probabilities, unexpected model versions, non-finite coordinates and degenerate boxes. A failed output is an abstention for every policy and remains in evaluation denominators. The selected Choice label is used only to route rejected cases to clarification or abstention; no interactive clarification was executed.')
 table('Policy definitions. All original thresholds and logistic coefficients remain frozen.', ['Policy','Score','Threshold'],[['A','Every valid parsed box','0'],['B','b','0.83'],['C','min(p,b)','0.83'],['D','min(p,u,b)','0.80'],['E','sigmoid(-4.496 + 2.509p + 2.696u + 0.600b)','0.761012'],['Presence','p','1.00'],['Uniqueness','u','1.00'],['Presence + uniqueness','min(p,u)','1.00']])
 p('E is an L2-regularized logistic regression on the three native scores, fitted to 79 valid development responses. There are no interaction features. Thresholds maximize attainable calibration coverage subject to coverage at most 30%, breaking ties toward the larger threshold. This is a coverage-selection rule, not a calibrated risk guarantee. No external labels are used to refit these original policies; Section 7 reports a separately named real-fitted diagnostic.')
 eq(r'\tau^*=\arg\max_{\tau}\{\widehat C_{cal}(\tau):\widehat C_{cal}(\tau)\leq0.30\}.', 'Threshold rule: maximize calibration coverage C_cal(t) subject to C_cal(t) <= 0.30.')
 p('Let y_i=1 only when the target is unique, the predicted center lies in its reference box, and intersection-over-union is at least 0.5. Let a_i(t)=1 for a valid output whose score exceeds the threshold; otherwise a_i=0. Conditional selective risk and marginal incorrect-action rate have different denominators:')
 eq(r'\widehat C(t)=\frac{1}{n}\sum_i a_i(t),\quad \widehat R(t)=\frac{\sum_i a_i(t)(1-y_i)}{\sum_i a_i(t)},\quad \widehat M(t)=\frac{1}{n}\sum_i a_i(t)(1-y_i).', 'Coverage = accepts / n; selective risk = incorrect accepts / accepts; marginal error = incorrect accepts / n.')
 p('Risk is undefined when there are no accepted cases; it is never reported as zero for an empty set. Presence calibration targets the human states unique or multiple; uniqueness calibration targets the unique state. Box and logistic calibration target the full primary correctness criterion. These distinct events must not be interpreted as interchangeable probabilities.')
 h('4. Data, annotation and provenance audit')
 p('The synthetic pilot has 25 templates in five layout families, eight state variants per template, and procedural geometry labels. Sidebar and kanban form development (80); tables form calibration (40); dialogs and toolbars form held-out test (80). Each template contributes three unique cases and one each of absent, multiple, occluded, offscreen and unclear. The synthetic experiment issued 200 calls without retries; 199 parsed successfully. One development zero-width box failed closed.')
 p('Two annotators independently labeled the same 951 cases without model predictions. They chose one of six target states, boxed all valid visible targets for unique/multiple cases, and recorded confidence. Both corrected exports pass structural validation. Status agreement is 759/951 (79.8%), Cohen\'s kappa is 0.613, and mean matched box IoU is 0.619 across 205 comparable cases. A deterministic rule flags status disagreement, box-count disagreement, matched IoU below 0.5, or low confidence. It flags 259 cases; reasons overlap. At the authors\' direction, the reviewer-approved Annotator B decision is final on flagged cases; the other 692 use consensus and matched-coordinate means where localizable.')
 p('An offline source audit finds that 200 of the 951 human-labeled cases reuse exactly the same screenshot bytes and instructions as the pilot: 80 development, 40 calibration and 80 held-out cases. They cannot supply independent external evidence. We therefore report all 951 only as a mixed descriptive cohort, and separately analyze the 751 ScreenSpot-v2-variant real-interface cases [8]. The subset analysis was added after inspecting the mixed results, so it is explicitly a post-hoc provenance correction, not a new preregistered experiment. The real subset consists of source-proposed negative instructions rechecked by humans; 131 are ultimately unique, demonstrating why automatic negative labels were insufficient. It is not the standard positive-target ScreenSpot-v2 test set.')
 table('Cohort composition by frozen human target state. No cases are dropped for schema errors.', ['Cohort','Unique','Absent','Multiple','Occluded','Offscreen','Unclear'],[['Real interfaces (751)',131,583,29,2,5,1],['Reused synthetic (200)',75,57,25,25,0,18],['Mixed total (951)',206,640,54,27,5,19]])
 fig('results/extended/dataset_flow.png','Figure 1. Annotation, source audit and evaluation flow. The real-only analysis separates reused synthetic material.',53)
 h('5. Evaluation and uncertainty protocol')
 p('The main experimental policy definitions remain unchanged. We report strict localization, center-only accuracy as a secondary metric, accepted-error risk, marginal error, state-specific acceptance and useful automation among unique cases. Confidence intervals use 2,000 nonparametric source-image cluster bootstrap resamples with seed 1729. Repeated instructions sharing a screenshot are resampled together. Each ratio is recomputed after resampling, with undefined replicates omitted and their count retained. Primary comparison tests flip paired source-cluster effects and use 10,000 randomizations; Holm adjustment covers the absent and multiple endpoints within each cohort.')
 p('The real-only comparisons and all new subgroup, calibration, entropy and curve analyses are exploratory. The absent-target adjusted p=0.0476 is borderline under this exploratory family and carries no central claim. Their two-endpoint Holm adjustment does not cover the full collection of diagnostic analyses. Percentile bootstrap intervals are descriptive and can be unreliable with few accepted cases. A zero-error bootstrap interval is degenerate because resampling cannot create unseen errors; it is not evidence of a true zero risk.')
 h('6. Results: real-interface usefulness and base localization')
 data=[]
 for n in ['B','C','D','E','uniqueness']:
  m=metric('real_751',n);data.append([n,m['accepted'],m['incorrect_accepted'],pct(m['coverage']),pct(m['selective_risk']),pct(m['unique_useful_automation_rate'])])
 table('Primary real-interface results: 751 cases, including 131 unique targets. Original thresholds remain frozen.', ['Policy','Accept','Errors','Coverage','Risk','Unique automation'],data)
 p('The mixed zero-error logistic result is dominated by reused synthetic inputs: 68 of its 69 accepted cases come from the reused group. On 751 real interfaces, E accepts one correct mobile case (0.13% coverage). D has 26 accepts and 16 errors (61.5% risk), versus 45 accepts and 28 errors for B (62.2% risk). Reducing the absolute number of errors through abstention does not establish lower conditional risk or useful transfer.')
 p('For real unique targets, strict localization is correct in 41/131 cases (31.3%); IoU at least 0.75 occurs in 12/131 (9.2%), and mean IoU is 0.327. The secondary center-only criterion is satisfied in 77/131 (58.8%). The large gap illustrates the sensitivity of apparent accuracy to the scoring rule. Box acceptance and safe selection should be evaluated separately.')
 table('Source-cluster 95% intervals on the real subset. Risk for E is not inferentially informative.', ['Policy','Coverage [95% CI]','Risk [95% CI]','Useful unique [95% CI]'],[[n,f"{pct(metric('real_751',n)['coverage'])} [{pct(s['confidence_intervals']['real_751'][n]['coverage']['low'])}, {pct(s['confidence_intervals']['real_751'][n]['coverage']['high'])}]",'not informative (1 accept)' if n=='E' else f"{pct(metric('real_751',n)['selective_risk'])} [{pct(s['confidence_intervals']['real_751'][n]['selective_risk']['low'])}, {pct(s['confidence_intervals']['real_751'][n]['selective_risk']['high'])}]",f"{pct(metric('real_751',n)['unique_useful_automation_rate'])} [{pct(s['confidence_intervals']['real_751'][n]['unique_useful_automation_rate']['low'])}, {pct(s['confidence_intervals']['real_751'][n]['unique_useful_automation_rate']['high'])}]"] for n in ['B','D','E']])
 p('On real absent targets, B accepts 15/583 and D 7/583, a case-weighted difference of -1.37 points (exploratory two-endpoint Holm-adjusted cluster-sign p=0.0476). On real multiple targets, acceptance changes from 1/29 to 0/29 (adjusted p=1.0). The much larger mixed multiple-target reduction, 21/54 to 0/54, comes mostly from reused synthetic examples. Its mixed-cohort significance does not establish a real-interface ambiguity result.')
 table('Additional real-subset 95% source-cluster intervals. A zero-error interval is degenerate, not a guarantee.', ['Metric','B estimate [95% CI]','D estimate [95% CI]'],[[label]+[f"{pct(metric('real_751',n)[key])} [{pct(s['confidence_intervals']['real_751'][n][key]['low'])}, {pct(s['confidence_intervals']['real_751'][n][key]['high'])}]" for n in ['B','D']] for key,label in [('marginal_incorrect_action_rate','Marginal incorrect-action rate'),('absent_false_accept_rate','Absent false-accept rate'),('ambiguous_wrong_accept_rate','Multiple wrong-accept rate')]])
 collection_file=ROOT/'results/iteration3/collection_summary.json'
 if collection_file.exists() and json.loads(collection_file.read_text()).get('jev_collection_state')=='COMPLETE':
  p('The completed repeat study reinforces this low-coverage result: across five original-prompt passes, D accepts 21-26 real cases with 57.1-71.4% conditional risk, while E accepts 0-1 case. Both prewritten prompt variants leave E with no accepts. These repetitions diagnose prediction stability; they do not multiply the number of independent source images. Appendix C.4 reports all pass metrics, failures and additional cost.')
 h('7. Diagnostics and mathematical decision analysis')
 p('Base localization and rejection quality are distinct. Under a center-only rule, real unique-target accuracy rises from 31.3% to 58.8%; IoU0.3 alone gives 48.9%. Among real cases both annotators label unique, only 61.2% of their boxes meet IoU0.5. These human agreement rates are a conditional reference, not an absolute performance ceiling. Full label/criterion sensitivity appears in Appendix C.1.')
 p('An empirical oracle bound makes the base-grounder limitation explicit. For fixed recorded boxes and labels, let pi_correct be the fraction of all cases with a strictly correct localization. Any gate has correct-acceptance coverage at most pi_correct. If its observed conditional error is at most alpha, its total acceptance coverage is at most pi_correct/(1-alpha). Here pi_correct=41/751, so even an oracle gate is bounded by about 6.1% coverage at alpha=0.10. This is a deterministic finite-cohort bound, not a population guarantee or human ceiling; a different grounder or scoring rule changes it.')
 eq(r'\widehat C_{TP}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_i a_i y_i\leq\widehat\pi_{correct},\quad\widehat R\leq\alpha\Longrightarrow\widehat C\leq\frac{\widehat\pi_{correct}}{1-\alpha}.','For fixed boxes and labels, correct accepted coverage is bounded by the observed localization prevalence; a risk-limited gate cannot exceed that prevalence divided by one minus its risk budget.')
 p('Original E has Brier loss 0.06125, worse than the retrospective constant-prevalence predictor at 0.05234 (Brier skill -0.170). Its low ECE therefore does not establish useful probability quality. B and E have nearly identical AUROC and overlapping source-cluster intervals; extra native signals do not show strong added discrimination on this real cohort. Appendix C.2 reports the full ranking and Brier analysis.')
 p('A source-separated fit/calibration/test diagnostic trains a new logistic score on real fitting data only. Test Brier loss improves from 0.07345 to 0.05822, but neither an empirical calibration-risk rule nor the fixed Learn-then-Test procedure finds a qualifying threshold at 10% accepted-error risk. This negative recovery result separates improved probability estimation from useful low-risk acceptance. The retrospective design and source-distribution assumptions preclude a new confirmatory guarantee; Appendix C.3 details the procedure.')

 p('The appendix reports desktop, mobile and web, status, target-area, element-type, instruction-length, annotator-confidence and label-provenance strata. Target size is reference-box area divided by screenshot area, defined only for unique cases: small below 0.1%, medium 0.1-1%, and large at least 1%. Confidence means the least confident of the two annotators. Sparse strata are descriptive and are not used to tune policies.')
 fig('results/extended/risk_coverage.png','Figure 2. Descriptive risk-coverage sweeps on mixed and real-only cohorts. Dots show the originally frozen operating points. Sweeps inspect test outcomes and cannot be used to select a validated deployment threshold.',76)
 p('For an exploratory entropy baseline, h=1-H(q)/log(3), where q is the three-option match-count distribution. A second baseline additionally requires the selected Choice to be one. Their thresholds use only original synthetic calibration outputs at the same 30% coverage rule, but these baseline designs were introduced after seeing transfer outcomes. Entropy alone selects an infinite threshold and accepts nothing because the tied high-score calibration mass exceeds 30%. Entropy-plus-one uses threshold 1 and accepts two real cases with one error. Low entropy can mean confidence in absence or multiplicity; it is not equivalent to confidence in a safe click.')
 eq(r'H(q)=-\sum_{k\in\{0,1,>1\}}q_k\log q_k,\qquad h(q)=1-\frac{H(q)}{\log 3}.','Choice entropy: H(q) = -sum q_k log q_k; confidence score h = 1 - H(q)/log(3).')
 fig('results/extended/calibration.png','Figure 3. Ten equal-width reliability bins, valid responses only. Different curves target different events, as defined in Section 3; they are not interchangeable calibration comparisons.',76)
 table('Real-subset calibration on 740 valid responses. Brier and ECE are descriptive, with ten fixed bins.', ['Score','Event','Brier','ECE'],[[n,s['calibration']['real_751'][n]['target'],f"{s['calibration']['real_751'][n]['brier']:.3f}",f"{s['calibration']['real_751'][n]['ece']:.3f}"] for n in ['presence','uniqueness','B','E']])
 p('As standard decision-theoretic framing rather than a methodological contribution, define a transparent utility: a correct accepted click earns one unit, an incorrect accepted click loses lambda units, and a rejection earns zero. This is a hypothetical sensitivity model, not measured harm. Dollar API cost and wall-clock latency are reported separately.')
 eq(r'\widehat U_\lambda=\frac{TP-\lambda FP}{n}=\widehat C\,[1-(1+\lambda)\widehat R].','Utility U_lambda = (correct accepts - lambda * incorrect accepts) / n = coverage * [1 - (1+lambda)*risk].')
 p('On real interfaces, U_B=(17-28 lambda)/751, U_D=(10-16 lambda)/751 and U_E=1/751. D exceeds B only when lambda > 7/12; E exceeds D only when lambda > 9/16. A completely abstaining policy has zero utility. These break-even points expose the coverage-risk trade-off rather than labeling one policy universally best.')
 eq(r'q(x)-\lambda[1-q(x)]\geq0\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad q(x)\geq\frac{\lambda}{1+\lambda}.','If q(x) were a calibrated safe-click probability, execute when q(x) >= lambda/(1+lambda).')
 p('The last inequality is a Bayes decision derivation under the stated utility. The native scores have not been shown to satisfy its calibration assumptions, so it supplies no guarantee for D or E. Under optimistic independent Bernoulli sampling, zero errors in m accepts yields one-sided 95% upper bound 1-0.05^(1/m). For the single real E acceptance this is 95%, demonstrating the weakness of that zero-error observation; source dependence makes even this illustration optimistic.')
 h('8. Same-input model comparison, latency and cost')
 if baseline.get('state')=='COMPLETE':
  p('The independent Qwen2.5-VL-7B baseline uses the same 951 prepared images and human references; input-image hashes and pinned model/protocol hashes match throughout. Primary accuracy uses only the 751 real cases, including 131 unique targets. Qwen uses Q4_K_M quantization through Ollama, a frozen forced-box JSON prompt and prompted confidence values; Jev uses its native typed interface. No Jev-trained logistic coefficients are applied to Qwen. These interface and deployment choices delimit the comparison.')
  mm=baseline['primary_metrics'];deploy=baseline['deployment_comparison']
  table('Observed same-input comparison. Accuracy denominators are 131 unique real targets; timing and failures use all 951 matched inputs. Local total economic cost is unmeasured.', ['Model / deployment','Strict','Center-only','Failures /951','Mean seconds','p95 seconds','API cost /951'],[[name+(' HTTP' if name=='Jev' else ' Q4 local'),pct(mm[name]['strict_correct']/131),pct(mm[name]['center_correct']/131),deploy[name]['failure_outputs'],f"{deploy[name]['latency_mean_ms']/1000:.3f}",f"{deploy[name]['latency_p95_ms']/1000:.3f}",f"${deploy[name]['api_cost_estimate_usd']:.4f}" if name=='Jev' else '$0 API; compute unpriced'] for name in ['Jev','Qwen']])
  fig('results/iteration3/same_data_comparison.png','Figure 4. Observed geometry under the fixed baseline interface. Right: Qwen-minus-Jev differences with 2,000 paired source-image bootstrap resamples. These exploratory intervals are unadjusted across criteria; hardware, quantization and interface effects are not separated.',76)
  difference=baseline['paired_geometry_comparison'];key='strict_unique_accuracy_difference_Qwen_minus_Jev';ci=difference['source_cluster_ci'][key]
  p(f"Strict accuracy is {pct(mm['Jev']['strict_correct']/131)} for Jev and {pct(mm['Qwen']['strict_correct']/131)} for Qwen. The paired Qwen-minus-Jev strict difference is {100*difference['estimates'][key]:.1f} percentage points (source-cluster 95% interval {100*ci['low']:.1f} to {100*ci['high']:.1f}). Center-only accuracy is closer, {pct(mm['Jev']['center_correct']/131)} versus {pct(mm['Qwen']['center_correct']/131)}; its difference interval reaches zero. The stricter result favors the tested Jev interface on this cohort, without establishing general model superiority.")
  p('A shared semantic rule accepts valid outputs only when the predicted match-count label is one. It accepts 194/751 Jev cases with 163 incorrect accepts, and 604/751 Qwen cases with 583 incorrect accepts. This deliberately simple common rule is separate from frozen policies B-E and shows that a semantic one label alone is insufficient for reliable acceptance. Prompted Qwen scores are not native calibrated probabilities.')
  p('The 62 Qwen failures are out-of-bounds or degenerate boxes and remain fail-closed; 57 occur in the real subset. Jev has 11 malformed outputs in the original matched 951 collection, all in the real subset. No output repair or selective rerun was applied. The comparison evaluates the specified forced-box interfaces; it does not isolate model weights, quantization, structured decoding or coordinate-prompt effects. Different model-specific optimization may change results.')
  p('Latency is wall-clock request-to-full-response time for each tested deployment. Hosted Jev and serial local Qwen use different hardware and execution stacks; their observed times are not a hardware-normalized speed comparison. Both include malformed-response time. Local Qwen hardware and electricity are unpriced, so zero API charges do not imply zero economic cost. Published model numbers and GPT-4o price scenarios remain historical context in Appendix D.')
 else:
  p('The independent Qwen2.5-VL baseline collection is incomplete; Appendix C.4 reports its status without a partial accuracy claim.')
 p(f"The external collection contains 951 HTTP-200 responses and 71 earlier credential rejections. Of the former, 940 parse and 11 fail closed: six invalid boxes and five invalid probability sums. Usage must be extracted from raw responses even when parsing fails. Total usage is {op['input_tokens']:,} input and {op['output_tokens']:,} output tokens. Under the recorded Jev input rate of $0.042 per million tokens, consistent with the provider listing [9], output tokens are listed as free [9]. Estimated cost is $0.134370, or about $0.0001413 per HTTP-200 response. This is a rate-based estimate rather than a provider invoice. Synthetic-study cost was approximately $0.019235. Mean valid-or-malformed HTTP-200 latency is 459 ms and p95 628 ms; including setup rejections gives 456 ms mean.")
 h('9. Discussion, limitations and research implications')
 p('The central result is the gap between authored-template discrimination and real-interface usefulness. Semantic confidence may successfully reject obvious synthetic duplicates yet fail to transfer to underspecified real instructions and small controls. A low marginal error can be obtained through almost universal abstention. The source audit shows why an apparently strong mixed-cohort result must be decomposed before a generalization claim is made.')
 p('Annotations remain a material limitation: status agreement is below 80%, and final labels for flagged cases follow reviewer-approved B rather than a separate third adjudicator. We cannot infer independent review reliability from this provenance alone. A blinded 100-case audit package (60 flagged and 40 consensus cases) is prepared for a human reviewer. No third-review results are claimed. Changing labels later requires a new dataset version and clearly marked sensitivity analysis.')
 p('The real cohort is skewed toward absence (583/751), and unique labels arise from rechecking proposed negative instructions. Mobile, web and desktop are represented, but rare offscreen/occluded strata are too small for stable conclusions. The public training data of Jev are not known, so training contamination cannot be ruled out. Repeated template screenshots are addressed by source-image clustering, but application-level dependence may remain. New subgroup diagnostics were planned after mixed outcomes were visible and have no global multiplicity correction.')
 p('The reviewer-driven expansion includes a five-pass Jev study with two prompt variants and a pinned local Qwen2.5-VL collection; completion and results are reported in Appendix C. Both repeatability and same-data baseline collection are complete. The single quantized baseline does not establish robustness across model families or deployment configurations. The present work supplies no formal risk control under distribution shift. Offline incorrect clicks are not measurements of actual user harm, and no real application action was executed. Stronger future evidence requires independent adjudication, new balanced real-interface cases and broader model/configuration coverage.')
 h('10. Ethics and reproducibility')
 p('API keys are environment variables and excluded from artifacts. Logs, frozen labels and policies remain unchanged; corrected accounting and post-hoc analyses are versioned separately. External images may contain names or other personal information; screenshots and full annotation exports should remain access-controlled until privacy and source-license review. Public reproducibility material is therefore a manifest/code package with a restricted-data acquisition note, not an unrestricted image redistribution. Author names, affiliations, venue declarations and a public repository URL remain submission-specific tasks.')
 p('The artifact contains raw synthetic and external API responses, label exports, source metadata, image-hash overlap audit, per-case decision ledger, full stratified CSVs, 2,000-resample intervals, source-cluster randomization tests, risk-coverage sweeps, reliability bins, cost assumptions, qualitative examples and a shared-content manuscript builder. The offline analysis never imports an API client. A reproduction script runs existing data only, with paid collection commands excluded.')
 h('11. Conclusion')
 p('Native uniqueness and presence signals substantially improve selective clicking on authored synthetic layouts, but this result does not establish real-interface usefulness. Removing 200 reused pilot cases reveals that the frozen logistic gate accepts just one of 751 real-interface cases, while three-signal conditional risk remains 61.5%. The defensible contribution is a transparent account of transfer failure, denominator-sensitive safety metrics and cohort provenance. The matched-input quantized Qwen baseline has weaker observed strict localization under the specified interface, while neither semantic one-label rule yields reliable acceptance. Broader model superiority, formal safety and practical utility remain unestablished.')
 h('Appendix A. Complete subgroup diagnostics')
